      "Drainage problems are often easiest to understand during or immediately after heavy rain. If it is safe to do so, take photos or video showing where water enters the property, where it ponds, and where it eventually leaves. Those observations can provide information that a dry-site visit may not reveal. Note whether the issue is new or has existed for years, and whether nearby construction or road work changed the flow. A contractor can use those details along with the existing elevations to discuss practical grading options. The goal should be controlled drainage across the property, not simply moving water from one unwanted spot to another. A thoughtful drainage plan should improve the problem area while still respecting the way runoff naturally moves beyond the work zone."
